+## Current activation (momentum cost-sensitivity on the collected universe — R-001, agent 02, run 43724282721, 2026-11-15)
+
+Bounded transaction-cost sensitivity for the admitted short-horizon momentum
+evidence (lookback 3/5/10). The check
+(`research/checks/momentum_cost_sensitivity.py`) runs the existing
+`stress_segments` walk-forward gate (train=252d/test=84d/warmup=60d/overlap=60d;
+4 contiguous volatility blocks; min 400 bars/segment) across the 10-asset
+universe under a predeclared cost grid — zero, the repository's existing
+realistic model (commission_per_trade=2.0, commission_per_share=0.003,
+slippage_cents=2.0, slippage_proportional=0.0005, from
+`examples/ma_crossover_real_data.py`), and a 2x conservative stress — with the
+matched coin-flip null costed identically. Artifact:
+`state/check_artifacts/momentum_cost_sensitivity_results.json`.
+
+The decisive finding is not the cost verdict but *what the signal trades*.
+`research/backtest/regime_stability.py::momentum_signals` computes
+`mean(log(closes[i-lookback+1:i+1]))` and goes long when that is > 0. For any
+price above 1 this is always positive, so the signal is a **static long
+(buy-and-hold)**, not a momentum rule: the lookback is inert. Measured on the
+full series, 9/10 assets have exactly 1 weight change over 4,465 bars
+(frac_long 0.9986-0.9993) and NVDA has 2 (frac_long 0.5841, an early sub-$1
+split-adjusted period). For AAPL/AMZN/TSLA the repo momentum medians are exactly
+identical to a fresh always-long (all +1) walk-forward. A textbook
+trailing-return momentum (sign of `log(c_t) - log(c_{t-lb})`) would trade ~500-
+1200 times (AAPL 799 sign changes); the repo formula trades once. The earlier
+"identical medians across lookbacks" that was read as horizon-stable magnitude is
+the signature of this inert lookback, not evidence of robustness.
+
+Consequences for the cost question (reference lookback 5):
+- All 10 assets classify COST_ROBUST by the predeclared absolute-net rule
+  (net cost-inclusive medians essentially unchanged from zero to 2x costs:
+  AAPL 0.0700/0.0700/0.0700, AMZN 0.0760/0.0760/0.0760, NVDA
+  0.0130/0.0120/0.0110, etc.), but the informative subset (signals that actually
+  trade, TRADING turnover > 3) is **empty**. The robustness is real but vacuous:
+  a position that never rebalances barely pays costs.
+- The matched-null gap *widens* with cost (e.g. AAPL +0.0587/+0.0642/+0.0702)
+  because the iid coin-flip null churns ~2,384-2,953 times per series versus 1-2
+  for momentum; cost stress penalises the null far more. The gap is therefore
+  confounded and is stored only as a secondary, clearly-labelled diagnostic — it
+  must not be read as the edge strengthening under costs.
+
+Verification: independent verifier `verify_momentum_cost_sensitivity.py` (5
+gates) all MATCH/PASS — fresh `walk_forward`+`noise_benchmark` per segment for
+AMZN/JPM across all lookbacks and cost levels (no `stress_segments`); independent
+static-signal confirmation (repo formula sign changes = 1; repo momentum medians
+== fresh always-long for AAPL/AMZN/TSLA); cost-verdict re-derivation for all
+10 assets x 3 lookbacks; zero-cost column cross-consistent with
+`momentum_lookback_sweep_results.json`; determinism. Manifest 10/10 OK, preflight
+PASSED, leakage PASS (AMZN/JPM). Regression suite: 168 tests, all passing.
+
+Assessment: the cost question is answered, but it dissolves the premise. The
+admitted "short-horizon momentum edge" is a static long / buy-and-hold drift, not
+a momentum strategy; its cost robustness is trivially high and carries no
+information about a tradable momentum edge. The same defect affects
+`mean_reversion_signals` (always short), so the reversal falsification is also
+"always-short loses." Both cells must be re-derived with a corrected return-based
+signal before any momentum/reversal claim is admitted. Recorded as a durable
+negative correction; the signal rule was NOT changed here (out of scope).
+
+---
+
